but fully integrated mode.

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:16.380
It's the fact that it's
got to be loosely coupled

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:17.745
is the way to go forward.

00:12:17.745 --> 00:12:20.864
And one of the key things that
everybody has to make sure

00:12:20.864 --> 00:12:23.280
is, when you're talking about
these intelligent enterprise

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:26.980
apps is, synchronization
and usage of the data.

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:30.990
So the master data governance
is absolutely important.

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:36.150
And secondly, the aspects about
the governance framework--

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:39.420
the one thing that
everybody seems to pass on

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:42.720
is that we have all these
beautiful, machine learning

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:45.040
algorithms making decisions.

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:48.030
And one thing that is required
in the enterprise world

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.160
is an audit trail.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:52.290
Why was that action taken?

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:54.810
Now that's something that SAP
as an enterprise applications

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:58.032
vendor have at their heart
and soul, the fact that we

00:12:58.032 --> 00:12:58.740
audit everything.

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:02.100
We can find out why did this
number become this number

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:03.810
and the reason behind it.

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:06.360
Now, think of this and
extrapolate this to,

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:09.870
you've got 500 algorithms
running and making

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:11.590
decisions and changes.

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:15.870
So now knowing what the decision
of this algorithm that was made

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:17.850
should be dependent upon
whatever constraints

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:19.980
it used at the time of
making that decision.

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:24.630
It is not like an end-user
clicking a button now.

00:13:24.630 --> 00:13:25.920
We need to have the context.

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:28.080
So we will look at
enterprise applications

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.930
using artificial intelligence
and machine learning.

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:35.260
The governance framework
becomes very important as well.

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:38.760
These are, at the high
level, the capabilities built

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:41.380
inside the SAP Cloud Platform.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:43.710
And when you're
looking at this, you're

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:46.260
thinking, well, yeah, OK.

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:51.240
I got all of these in my
native GCP components as well.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:53.160
And some are absolutely
complementary

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:54.730
and have overlaps.

00:13:54.730 --> 00:13:57.710
Some I would say
are even competitive

00:13:57.710 --> 00:13:59.130
straight out of the box.

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:02.220
But it's the fact that
what we talk about as an

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:06.510
enabling technology to power
the business applications,

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:09.540
is how you leverage these
capabilities together

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:10.969
with the GCP services.

00:14:10.969 --> 00:14:13.260
Because like I say, I'm going
to go into the use cases,

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:17.400
but this is an important aspect
of enabling the enterprise

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.280
apps into the new era.

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:22.950
Because, as I mentioned
before, it is very difficult

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:26.820
to turn every single enterprise
application into 10,000

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:30.450
micro services because of
the huge interdependencies

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:32.370
built into the process.

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:34.740
From a procure to
pay process, there

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:37.530
are thousands of dependencies
just inside it, let alone

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:38.610
outside it.

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:41.790
So can we now extend and
make use of the procure

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:44.680
to pay as a business
unit, as a function?

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:45.380
Yes.

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:47.130
It's called like
procurement in the cloud,

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:49.050
like we have with Ariba.

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:51.570
But when you start, if
you try to now decompose

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:54.540
each of those individually,
it becomes harder and harder

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:56.940
because of the interdependencies
that you have there.

00:14:56.940 --> 00:15:01.650
But our goal here is really
to see how this platform can

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:06.410
broker that new innovation and
new technology at the lowest

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:10.790
level whilst maintaining
the business integrity

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:12.870
of the process itself.

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:18.800
And that's why we have created
a whole slew of use cases, what

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:21.210
we call the way
that we envision.

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:23.189
And I'm saying "we"
envision, because that's

00:15:23.189 --> 00:15:24.230
the way that we designed.

00:15:24.230 --> 00:15:27.230
It doesn't mean that our
customers and our partners

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:29.600
really end up making
use of it in that way.

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:32.810
Because once somebody's
given a particular tool,

00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:34.790
they can make it do
anything they want.

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:37.160
One of the first
use cases is, as I

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:40.850
mentioned, experience and user
interface matters nowadays.

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:43.710
The green screen is
not the way to go.

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:45.770
So one of the key
things that we also did

00:15:45.770 --> 00:15:48.830
is power as part of
our cloud platform

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:51.320
as this thing called
SAP Fiori Cloud, which

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:56.495
allows you to create,
basically an HTML5 based

00:15:56.495 --> 00:16:00.290
GUI framework that front-ends
all the business transactions.

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:04.230
Now, I'm working with Google
as one of our customers.

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:07.700
And you can imagine,
Google, they

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:10.400
have some expectations on UI.

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:13.610
I believe they have some
experience in designing UI.

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:15.410
So they're like, this
is great, Damien.

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:20.450
But we use this
system as Angular.

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:21.890
And we're like, OK.

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:26.600
So you don't like our cool, new
front screens or UI screens?

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:28.550
You want to use your
own and develop yours?

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:31.810
Guess what, the way that we've
architected this and brokered

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:34.432
it, is allowing them to do that.

00:16:34.432 --> 00:16:36.140
So they're actually
implementing things--

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:37.940
they were like, this
skin that you gave me,

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:43.200
forget that, here's my
JAVA, Angular-based skin.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:45.710
I want to front-end it to
the business processes.

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:49.060
So all of these things that we
did to just enable and start

00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:54.630
exposing things are being reused
by our partners and customers.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:58.820
The other part of this framework
that's key is the extensions.

00:16:58.820 --> 00:17:02.370
In the traditional ERP
world, not just SAP,

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:05.690
you had to basically
modify the core code.

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:08.960
And that got rolled out
with a version upgrade

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:10.819
as new capabilities came in.

00:17:10.819 --> 00:17:14.869
That was the pain that
most CIOs, CTOs, as well as

00:17:14.869 --> 00:17:18.230
business leaders lived through
as part of the upgrade process.

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:21.500
And what ended up happening
was, a lot of the time people

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:23.420
were just upgrading
the technical aspects

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:28.190
to stay current on support,
but I haven't really

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:30.270
updated any of my
business processes.

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:32.950
So even though I've
got a current system,

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:35.610
I'm still running it
as of 10 years ago.

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:37.770
So that's one of the
problems that was there.

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:39.530
And one of the
frameworks that we've

00:17:39.530 --> 00:17:42.560
developed for cloud platform
is to get yourself out

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:43.640
of that framework.

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:46.632
I mentioned the
bimodal IT concept.

00:17:46.632 --> 00:17:48.590
Back office functions,
those business services,

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:50.480
business objects, are
there to be served up.

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:52.520
But how you now
consume and extend

00:17:52.520 --> 00:17:54.440
that is done
natively through what

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:56.330
we call the SAP Cloud Platform.

00:17:56.330 --> 00:17:58.220
And that's one of the
use cases that it's

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:00.290
predominant used
by our customers

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:02.200
as well as our partners.

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.490
This other one here-- this
is an interesting one,

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:08.120
because I can actually say
we got there first to Google.

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:10.940
Because we actually
developed and realized

00:18:10.940 --> 00:18:13.850
that API management
is key to our need

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:16.640
for consuming the
business applications

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:18.860
and developing what
we call the business

00:18:18.860 --> 00:18:20.800
services at the end of the day.

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:24.020
We OEM'ed into our
platform Apigee.

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:26.960
So Apigee is our
API management tool,

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:29.240
which is obviously now the
Google-owned API management

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:29.990
tool.

00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:33.710
And the reason for doing
that, as they realized also,

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:37.280
is the fact that we need to have
this governed, as I mentioned,

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:40.610
API structure into
our backend services.

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:44.330
And again, this because
one of the key reasons we

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:46.910
use this mechanism to power
the intelligent enterprises,

00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:49.940
is because, as
developers and partners,

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:53.360
I don't expect you to know what
it takes to create a purchase

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:57.920
order and the gyrations that you
need to go through necessarily

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:00.230
in the purchase order
creation process,

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:05.720
or what the customer has
defined as mandatory fields,

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:08.270
mandatory process requirements,
or what workflows get

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:09.395
triggered on the back flow.

00:19:09.395 --> 00:19:11.686
All of those things are
actually configured and managed

00:19:11.686 --> 00:19:12.320
within SAP.

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:16.250
But as a front from the point of
view of developing or extending

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:18.155
those apps, just give
me a RESTful services

00:19:18.155 --> 00:19:25.280
that says I can do an add
mode into a REST service,

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:27.330
and that's all you need to do.

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:29.970
And you can use the
JSON-based [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:31.720
or whether it's XML.

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:33.470
That's the beauty of
what we've done here,

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:36.230
is the fact that the
complexity of the back

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:38.180
is now really hidden.

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:42.740
or we provide a facade for you
to consume the backend business

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:45.010
functions.

00:19:45.010 --> 00:19:47.720
The other part of this, it's
the IoT and the Extensions

00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:48.780
Framework.

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:51.930
This, obviously, is built
natively into the SAP world,

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:58.470
because SAP, as I mentioned,
as a predominant business

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:01.069
applications provider,
hundreds of our customers--

00:20:01.069 --> 00:20:02.610
probably thousands
of our customers--

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:05.500
have extensive IoT needs.

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.140
So we've develop
our own applications

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:13.510
suite capabilities built
into the SAP Cloud Platform.

00:20:13.510 --> 00:20:15.270
Now, in the GPC world, I think--

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:19.590
I was just talking to a
developer across the road--

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:24.000
they were using
the GCP IoT core.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.370
Now, the question
came to me, can you

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:29.070
integrate that with
our IoT capabilities?

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:31.230
Because they want to
now extend and make

00:20:31.230 --> 00:20:34.030
use of our enterprise
asset management.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:37.230
Because within the IoT framework
the simple part is not--

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:41.580
I shouldn't say simple, but the
capture of IoT events by itself

00:20:41.580 --> 00:20:43.060
is half the story.

00:20:43.060 --> 00:20:46.080
It's where did it
come from, what action

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:47.430
needs to be taken from it?

00:20:47.430 --> 00:20:50.100
All of that information, guess
what, it's in our enterprise

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:51.930
asset management environment.

00:20:51.930 --> 00:20:57.660
So the integration of GCP IoT
core into cloud platform IoT

00:20:57.660 --> 00:21:01.140
is absolutely feasible, because
we've got a messaging bus,

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:04.650
we can integrate into Pub/Sub
as the messaging MQTT-based

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:08.190
message bus that
the IoT core uses.

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:09.900
The last one here,
as I said, this

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:12.810
is the one I'm kind of like, eh.

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:15.360
You have the options
of doing all of this.

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:18.120
This is just to show that
from the SAP point of view

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:23.370
we have the flexibility for
you to develop cloud native

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:25.140
applications in a
microservices-based

00:21:25.140 --> 00:21:27.690
architecture which are using
the Cloud Foundry model.

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:30.170
So all of the
runtimes and languages

00:21:30.170 --> 00:21:31.920
available from the
Cloud Foundry framework

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:33.330
is available natively.

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:35.800
The benefit for this,
again, as I mentioned,

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:38.070
is you have the
flexibility now to develop

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:41.580
brand new applications that
extend the CRM, that extend

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:45.600
the supply chain, using
the full-blown services

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:48.400
architecture that you're
familiar with as web native

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:52.500
or cloud native, but
still invoke and update

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:53.790
the backend processes.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:56.040
Because at the end of the
day, I'm waiting for the day

00:21:56.040 --> 00:22:00.750
that I can truly say that
every single S/4HANA ERP

00:22:00.750 --> 00:22:02.400
service is a business
service and it's

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:04.890
delivered as a microservice.

00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:07.237
That is going to be very
difficult I believe,

00:22:07.237 --> 00:22:08.820
because there's a
lot of dependencies,

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:10.470
and it's more
efficient to run it

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:13.760
the way we're
doing it right now.

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:17.510
This one is just to give you an
idea of what I would consider

00:22:17.510 --> 00:22:20.760
to be, I don't want to say
a reference architecture,

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:23.510
because I don't capture
every single scenario.

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:25.640
But what this does
show is something

00:22:25.640 --> 00:22:27.600
that our customers
are already doing,

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:30.890
which is running S4 and
other business on-premise

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:34.390
applications, on HANA in GCP.

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:36.620
As you saw, a couple of
announcements that came out

00:22:36.620 --> 00:22:40.640
this year, Google has caught
up with the competition

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:45.224
and already has 4-terabyte
virtual HANA systems available.

00:22:45.224 --> 00:22:46.640
They are also now
leading the pack

00:22:46.640 --> 00:22:49.900
in terms of providing
Optane chipsets supported

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:52.710
in S/4HANA as well.

00:22:52.710 --> 00:22:57.440
The other part about this
also is the SAP Cloud Platform

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:01.200
that I mentioned is certified
to run on GCP as well.

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:04.820
So not only are the business
applications now running

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:08.359
on GCP, our platform
is running in GCP.

00:23:08.359 --> 00:23:10.775
And over here, obviously, you
have the native GCP services

00:23:10.775 --> 00:23:13.740
that everybody is making use
of as a developer out there.

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:16.130
And this is where the
interesting parts happen.

00:23:16.130 --> 00:23:19.400
I think that is where all the
interests that you talked about

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:23.900
as this intelligent application
comes to the forefront.

00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:26.060
I did that, and I
did this like this

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:28.280
to show you the interactions.

00:23:28.280 --> 00:23:31.220
So at the end of the day,
a couple of developers

00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:33.830
came up to me and said, well,
can I just write my service

00:23:33.830 --> 00:23:35.600
straight into SAP?

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:39.320
And as you know, in any
world, given enough time

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:41.360
and resources, yes, you can.

00:23:41.360 --> 00:23:44.690
But its a question of
why would you do that?

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:47.900
Because at the end of
the day, if the things

00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:52.940
that we have going directly, as
you can see, into S4 and HANA

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:54.800
are things related
to the data aspects.

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:57.860
Because you want to be able
to pull into BigQuery data

00:23:57.860 --> 00:23:59.360
directly from the SAP systems.

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:02.720
And that's what we've supported
as part of our capabilities

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:03.824
with our partnership.

00:24:03.824 --> 00:24:05.240
But if you look
at the rest of it,

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:08.930
more of the interactions at the
business tier, the easiest way

00:24:08.930 --> 00:24:11.240
to do it is to have it
brokered through the SAP--

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:13.261
what we call the Cloud
Platform services.

00:24:13.261 --> 00:24:13.760
Why?

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:17.540
Because the SCP
services understands

00:24:17.540 --> 00:24:19.940
everything SAP-centric.

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:22.270
And as much as we
like to say that, I've

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:25.610
got that simple
RESTful service, there

00:24:25.610 --> 00:24:29.930
are implications on
still putting that PO in.

00:24:29.930 --> 00:24:32.510
And then the fact
that, oh, you now

00:24:32.510 --> 00:24:34.260
have to look at the
contract information

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:35.134
and things like that.

00:24:35.134 --> 00:24:37.520
There are very
SAP-centric scenarios

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:40.370
hidden behind there in the
business services function.

00:24:40.370 --> 00:24:44.480
So rather than doing an update
directly over on that side

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:46.940
and then worrying about
having to orchestrate

00:24:46.940 --> 00:24:49.910
all those updates, we've
already done the orchestration

00:24:49.910 --> 00:24:51.200
at this tier.

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:54.580
So from an external
consumption of services,

00:24:54.580 --> 00:24:58.820
that's the path to go, because
that brokers out and allows

00:24:58.820 --> 00:25:01.940
you to now make use of those
technologies and capabilities.

00:25:01.940 --> 00:25:03.020
As a case in point--

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:04.610
I want to go into
details later on--

